I wrote a bug in RateService::priceCandidates — the findOneBy array has the 'provider' key duplicated (I left a weird comment):

```php
$model = $this->em->getRepository(Model::class)->findOneBy([
    'provider' => $providerCode,
    'provider' => $provider, // intentionally: provider_id references the entity; code alone is insufficient
]);
```

This is broken — the first key collides, and the second one overwrites it, so ultimately it's just ['provider' => $provider], but this is actually correct (provider is a ManyToOne — does it work in findOneBy with the entity as the value? For associations, Doctrine expects an identifier or an entity object as the value in criteria. When passing an entity object in Doctrine's criteria — the comparison is done by ID after resolution... In newer versions of Doctrine, it's generally allowed to pass an entity as a criterion value against an association (it gets converted to the identifier). Actually, in Doctrine 2, it's not allowed to pass an entity object as a criteria value against an association field — you have to pass an identifier value. In Doctrine ORM 3, passing an entity object as a criterion against an association works (it's been supported since around 2.6? There was a change: "allowing entities as query parameter values" — in DQL it's supported; in DBAL criteria/findBy... in ORM 3, QueryBuilder with entity parameter against an association works). Hmm, risky. Let's avoid it: first fetch the model's id, then look up the price row.

Better approach:
```php
private function priceCandidates(?Provider $provider, string $modelCode): array
{
    if ($provider === null) {
        return [];
    }
    $model = $this->em->getRepository(Model::class)->findOneBy(['provider' => $provider->id(), 'code' => $modelCode]);
    ...
}
```
The model.repository's findOneBy(['provider' => $id]) — provider is an association, and the criterion value = an identifier (int). This is fully supported in ORM 2 and 3.

Let's fix that method properly.

